WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.435
-Our next guest is
an Emmy Award-winning writer

00:00:02.435 --> 00:00:03.903
and actress
you know from her work

00:00:03.903 --> 00:00:05.939
on "Saturday Night Live"
and the shows

00:00:05.939 --> 00:00:08.141
"Mapleworth Murders"
and "Girls5eva."

00:00:08.141 --> 00:00:11.110
She stars in "A.P. Bio," which
is streaming now on Peacock.

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:12.244
Let's take a look.

00:00:12.244 --> 00:00:14.380
-Did a tornado really kill
your grandfather?

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:16.382
-My grandfathers.

00:00:16.382 --> 00:00:17.850
It killed both of them.

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:19.819
They went out to distract it.

00:00:19.819 --> 00:00:23.356
So me and my meemaws could
get in the root cellar.

00:00:23.356 --> 00:00:25.759
And it scooped 'em up
and slammed 'em together

00:00:25.759 --> 00:00:27.593
like a pair of dirty flip-flops.

00:00:27.593 --> 00:00:29.762
And then I turned back to look.

00:00:29.762 --> 00:00:33.299
They popped like two water
balloons full of SpaghettiOs.

00:00:33.299 --> 00:00:35.467
They popped my pop-pops!

00:00:35.467 --> 00:00:39.806
And now, it's coming back
to get me, to finish the job.

00:00:39.806 --> 00:00:42.741
-Please welcome back to the show
one of the funniest people

00:00:42.741 --> 00:00:44.910
on the planet, our very good
friend Paula Pell.

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:48.514
Hello, Paula!
-Hi!

00:00:48.514 --> 00:00:51.350
-It's so good to see you.
You're looking resplendent.

00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:53.686
You are looking dressed the way

00:00:53.686 --> 00:00:56.522
an Emmy-nominated actress
should look.

00:00:56.522 --> 00:00:57.990
-Well, thank you so much.

00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:02.528
I wanted to zip up my Zoom
and just wear a little bit of

00:01:02.528 --> 00:01:06.599
sparkles for you, and I feel --
I can't believe we're having

00:01:06.599 --> 00:01:09.569
such an orthopedic episode of
your show,

00:01:09.569 --> 00:01:13.773
because of Amanda
with her pickleball shoulder,

00:01:13.773 --> 00:01:15.674
and I just got
a knee replacement

00:01:15.674 --> 00:01:18.278
because I have B.J. knee.

00:01:18.278 --> 00:01:25.985
[ Laughter ]

00:01:25.985 --> 00:01:28.654
-I'm so -- I'm so sorry.

00:01:28.654 --> 00:01:30.055
That just got diagnosed?

00:01:30.055 --> 00:01:31.724
-I only kneel on one knee.

00:01:31.724 --> 00:01:33.226
It's very formal when I do it.

00:01:33.226 --> 00:01:35.194
It's very seldom I do it,
I'm gonna be honest.

00:01:35.194 --> 00:01:37.463
-I think that's
the British style, right?

00:01:37.463 --> 00:01:40.033
One knee? That's a BBJ?

00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:44.637
[ Laughter ]

00:01:44.637 --> 00:01:48.775
-This also is the start of an
homage to Broadway coming back,

00:01:48.775 --> 00:01:52.044
which I'm so excited about,
and also my favorite

00:01:52.044 --> 00:01:56.015
New York memory, which was
going into therapy one time

00:01:56.015 --> 00:02:00.153
and some man coming down
the stairs from this building.

00:02:00.153 --> 00:02:03.022
And he had sort of
a sparkle scarf on,

00:02:03.022 --> 00:02:05.858
and he clearly had been
at voice lessons.

00:02:05.858 --> 00:02:09.528
And he was in the hall,
which had good acoustics,

00:02:09.528 --> 00:02:10.830
and he was singing...

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:16.202
♪ Give them
the old razzule dazzule ♪

00:02:16.202 --> 00:02:18.037
[ Laughter ]

00:02:18.037 --> 00:02:21.007
And it was clear to me that
the teacher had told him

00:02:21.007 --> 00:02:22.775
that he was swallowing
his vowels, you know?

00:02:22.775 --> 00:02:27.046
Because in choir they tell you,
like, "razzle, drazzle, dazzle."

00:02:27.046 --> 00:02:30.649
You have to ♪ razzle, dazzle ♪

00:02:30.649 --> 00:02:32.585
You create space in there.

00:02:32.585 --> 00:02:35.321
-Now, did you -- when you were
looking for a therapist,

00:02:35.321 --> 00:02:36.756
did you try to find one

00:02:36.756 --> 00:02:38.991
that was in a building
with a voice coach?

00:02:38.991 --> 00:02:41.093
-You know, in New York,
you don't have a choice.

00:02:41.093 --> 00:02:42.695
[ Laughter ]
You might be in there

00:02:42.695 --> 00:02:45.064
and someone's cooking pierogies
next door.

00:02:45.064 --> 00:02:47.066
[ Laughter ]
It's just -- there's a --

00:02:47.066 --> 00:02:50.770
There's just a weird amalgam of
people in New York apartments.

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:54.173
-I want to point out
that your Emmy nomination

00:02:54.173 --> 00:02:56.509
is for "Mapleworth Murders."

00:02:56.509 --> 00:02:58.477
There you are with
also Emmy-nominated

00:02:58.477 --> 00:03:00.313
and "Late Night" writer
John Lutz.

00:03:00.313 --> 00:03:01.481
-John Lutz.

00:03:01.481 --> 00:03:03.883
-And so exciting that
"A.P. Bio" is back.

00:03:03.883 --> 00:03:06.752
Helen is one of my favorite
characters on TV.

00:03:06.752 --> 00:03:08.988
An optimist. Wouldn't you
describe her as an optimist?

00:03:08.988 --> 00:03:11.723
-Yes, an optimist.
I love doing Helen.

00:03:11.723 --> 00:03:16.328
She reminds me a lot of
my Midwestern roots of my --

00:03:16.328 --> 00:03:21.066
of all the -- similar to
the South, of women that smile

00:03:21.066 --> 00:03:22.835
and completely lie to you.

00:03:22.835 --> 00:03:25.871
And so, my grandma, you know,

00:03:25.871 --> 00:03:30.610
she just always was
full of enthusiasm and cheer,

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:34.981
and then you'd get
little moments of truth.

00:03:34.981 --> 00:03:37.016
Like she used to --
we used to -- I used to take her

00:03:37.016 --> 00:03:40.419
to a little diner called
the Pine Cone.

00:03:40.419 --> 00:03:43.622
"Oh, God, and how, let's go to
the Pine Cone."

00:03:43.622 --> 00:03:45.792
We'd go to the Pine Cone
and get soup.

00:03:45.792 --> 00:03:48.695
Raviolis, they'd call them,
which is like tortellini soup.

00:03:48.695 --> 00:03:51.564
"Oh, is this soup ever good!

00:03:51.564 --> 00:03:52.965
Oh, and how!"

00:03:52.965 --> 00:03:54.367
And she'd call the manager over

00:03:54.367 --> 00:03:56.436
to specifically tell him
how good the soup is.

00:03:56.436 --> 00:03:57.937
She had her little saltines.

00:03:57.937 --> 00:04:02.775
"Oh, this soup is j--
and the broth, oh, my God."

00:04:02.775 --> 00:04:04.744
And then we'd leave,
and in the parking lot,

00:04:04.744 --> 00:04:06.179
she'd go,
"I didn't care for that soup."

00:04:06.179 --> 00:04:08.615
[ Laughter ]

00:04:08.615 --> 00:04:13.119
'Cause, you know, so Helen has
that, like, very dark underbelly

00:04:13.119 --> 00:04:15.221
that we're seeing more and more
in each season,

00:04:15.221 --> 00:04:19.091
where she's kind of full of it,
in terms of her --

00:04:19.091 --> 00:04:23.129
you know, she's a very
suppressed, more dark soul

00:04:23.129 --> 00:04:26.232
that's always laced in cheer,

00:04:26.232 --> 00:04:29.168
because that keeps her
wanting to live.

00:04:29.168 --> 00:04:31.470
-We have certainly been
enjoying the journey.

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:33.506
And then a really fun thing
that happened this year

00:04:33.506 --> 00:04:38.811
is your real-life wife, Janine,
played one of Helen's hook-ups.

00:04:38.811 --> 00:04:41.046
That must have been both nice

00:04:41.046 --> 00:04:44.383
and then it actually happened
on another show this year.

00:04:44.383 --> 00:04:45.985
-Yes. Two shows.

00:04:45.985 --> 00:04:48.554
People can't stop visualizing us
together.

00:04:48.554 --> 00:04:50.122
We must have
really good chemistry.

00:04:50.122 --> 00:04:53.626
We should probably meet
and get to know each other.

00:04:53.626 --> 00:04:56.995
It was super funny whenever
I shot with her this year,

00:04:56.995 --> 00:05:00.866
because both times
it was of a sexual nature

00:05:00.866 --> 00:05:02.668
and a romantic nature.

00:05:02.668 --> 00:05:06.272
And it was so funny because
you forget that not everyone

00:05:06.272 --> 00:05:07.873
in the crew knows
what's going on.

00:05:07.873 --> 00:05:10.142
You know, the writers knew
and the producers knew that

00:05:10.142 --> 00:05:12.778
that was my wife,
but a lot of the crew didn't.

00:05:12.778 --> 00:05:16.382
And so we're, like, you know,
making out in a bar

00:05:16.382 --> 00:05:20.553
and going into the bathroom
to hook up, and in between,

00:05:20.553 --> 00:05:21.920
you know, I hadn't seen her
in a while

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:23.722
'cause I was in California,
shooting.

00:05:23.722 --> 00:05:26.792
And so the two of us were,
like, very affectionate.

00:05:26.792 --> 00:05:28.761
I mean, we weren't,
like, tonguing each other,

00:05:28.761 --> 00:05:31.129
but we were just, like,
you know, being cozy

00:05:31.129 --> 00:05:33.032
and talking in little whispers
and everything.

00:05:33.032 --> 00:05:37.203
And then I'd look around and
people are like, "It's 2021.

00:05:37.203 --> 00:05:41.140
Like, Paula needs to cool it on
the special guests on the show.

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:44.077
Like, just meet her outside
the production, you know?"

